 ## Lemma 8 — adaptive probe bit bounds must be conditional
 
-Let `Theta` be the decision-relevant target and let an adaptive procedure choose
-probe `P_i` from history `H_{i-1}`, observe `Y_i`, and extend the history.  The
-chain rule gives
-
-```text
-I(Theta;Y_1,...,Y_n | P_1,...,P_n as generated)
- = sum_i I(Theta;Y_i | H_{i-1},P_i)
-```
-
-under the usual representation of the adaptive transcript.
-
-Hence if every legal probe satisfies the **conditional** bound
+Fix a finite number `n` of probe slots and define
+`H_i=(H_{i-1},P_i,Y_i)`.  Initial history `H_0` records all initial information
+and any independent algorithm random seed used by the adaptive policy.
+Assume probe selection adds no target information beyond that history:
+
+```text
+I(Theta;P_i | H_{i-1}) = 0.
+```
+
+Then the ordinary chain rule gives
+
+```text
+I(Theta;H_n | H_0)
+ = sum_i I(Theta;P_i,Y_i | H_{i-1})
+ = sum_i I(Theta;Y_i | H_{i-1},P_i).
+```
+
+The second equality expands each increment and uses the displayed independence
+condition.  Conditioning on all generated probe choices at once is different:
+future choices may encode past answers and remove their information.
+
+Hence if every increment satisfies the **conditional** bound
 
 ```text
 I(Theta;Y_i | H_{i-1},P_i) <= b,
 ```
 
-then the whole transcript carries at most `nb` bits about `Theta`.
+then the transcript adds at most `nb` bits beyond `H_0`.  If `H_0` is
+independent of `Theta`, this also bounds full-transcript information.
+No random or unbounded stopping-time extension is asserted here.
 
 A bound only on marginal `I(Theta;Y_i)` is insufficient because observations can
 be individually weak or redundant/masked while becoming informative jointly.
